Measurement tools for the numbers in ../FINDINGS.md. Each exists because a
naive grep produced a WRONG answer first; the wrong answer and its control are
recorded in FINDINGS.md's harness-bugs section.

strip.py <file>
    Blank out C comments while preserving line count and line numbering.
    Needed because `grep -v '\*'` (used to exclude pointer declarations when
    hunting by-value `struct mbuf`) also excluded any line whose COMMENT
    contained a '*', so a probe with two by-value declarations reported one.
    Control: /tmp/nc_mbuf/probe.c -- must report 2 by-value hits after
    stripping, and the pointer declaration must not appear.
    Also asserted: output line count == input line count, 31/31 files.

fnbody.py <stripped-file> <function>
    Extract one top-level C function body by brace matching.
    Needed because a line-range scan after each splnet() ran past the end of
    the function into the NEXT function's DEFINITION and scored it as a CALL,
    reporting 3 false splnet nests. The real count is 1.
    Controls: (positive) a body that really does call another region's
    function is found -- ieee80211_newstate -> ieee80211_free_allnodes;
    (negative) ieee80211_clean_cached's extracted body provably does NOT
    mention ieee80211_clean_nodes, whose definition immediately follows it.

cryptocount.py
    Count crypto primitive calls with MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE family patterns.
    Needed because \b-anchored patterns for rc4_* and arc4random* overlap, so
    a per-family sum double-counted and three successive drafts of the same
    sentence carried three different totals. Answer: 29 distinct primitives,
    94 calls.

Usage: run from a directory of comment-stripped sources, e.g.

    mkdir /tmp/n80_stripped
    for f in orig/*.c orig/*.h; do
        python3 tools/strip.py "$f" > /tmp/n80_stripped/$(basename $f)
    done
    cd /tmp/n80_stripped && python3 /path/to/tools/cryptocount.py
